<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

<h3>Is real-ear measurement uncomfortable or risky?</h3>
<p>It’s typically quick and comfortable. The probe is a soft, hair-thin tube placed a short, measured distance into the ear canal. Your clinician checks your ear first to make sure it’s safe. If you feel discomfort, they’ll reposition or stop.</p>

<h3>Do I still need REM if I’m buying over-the-counter (OTC) hearing aids?</h3>
<p>OTC devices can help for perceived mild-to-moderate loss, but they’re usually self-fit without verification. Some audiologists offer service packages to perform REM on OTC devices that allow clinician programming. If not possible, consider in-situ tests and outcome measures as a partial check.</p>

<h3>How often should REM be done?</h3>
<p>At initial fitting, after significant adjustments, and when your hearing changes. Many people benefit from a REM check at the first follow-up to confirm fine-tunes stayed on target.</p>

<h3>Can REM help with tinnitus?</h3>
<p>REM isn’t a tinnitus treatment, but accurate amplification can reduce listening strain and improve access to environmental sound, which some people find helps. If tinnitus bothers you, ask an audiologist about a full management plan.</p>


